Keywords: Persistent chemiluminescence; Nano/bio-enzymes; Local tandem catalysis; Porphyrin metal-organic framework; Chemiluminescence imaging assay
As chemiluminescence (CL) is excited by chemical reactions rather than excimer sources such as lasers and power supplies, it possesses advantages of high sensitivity, simple instrument, easy operation and low cost, and has been applied widely in chemical and biological fields.1,2 Especially, CL is attracted attention in imaging analysis because it performs high signal-tobackground ratio owing to no interferences of light source and autofluorescence.3-5 At present, the most developed and used CL systems are enzyme-involved CL reactions, including luminol–oxidant–horseradish peroxidase $(\mathrm{HRP})^{6,7}$ and dioxetane–alkaline phosphatase (ALP)8 CL systems. However, the CL intensity and emission time of these enzyme-based CL systems suffer from the poor stability and difficult modification of natural enzymes, limiting their application in bioimaging assays.
Besides, the peroxyoxalate ester CL system3,9-11 and Schaap’s dioxetane CL system5,12-14 have been designed to produce intensive and grow-type CL emission for in vivo imaging analysis. The former relies on the oxidation of peroxyoxalate esters with hydrogen peroxide $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2})$ to produce electronically excited 1,2-dioxetanedione intermediates which subsequently transfer energy to the fluorophore for luminescence. In order to achieve strong and long-lasting CL emission, different nanoparticles aggregating oxalate and fluorophore have been prepared. However, the lifetime of these CL nanoparticles (NP) is only tens of minutes. The Schaap’s dioxetane CL system is based on the chemiexcitation of adamantylidene−dioxetane through the removal of a protecting group by response molecules. In this case, because the CL efficiency of Schaap’s dioxetane is extremely low in aqueous conditions, phenoxy-dioxetane or dioxetane−fluorophore probes should be synthesized for bioassay.
Recently, glow-type micro/nanomaterial-based CL systems also have been proposed with slow-diffusion-controlled heterogeneous catalytic mechanism. In this kind of CL systems, a porous micro/nanomaterial embedding with catalyst (and one CL reagent) is required.15,16 The porous structure of the micro/nanomaterial causes the slow diffusion of CL reagent (or the coreactant), leading to the long-lasting CL emission. In addition, the high loading amount of catalyst in micro/nanomaterials results in the strong CL signal. The most typical example is the chitosan hydrogel doped with CL reagent N-(4-aminobutyl)-N-ethylisoluminol and catalyst $C0^{2+}$ , which can exhibit a visible luminescence with hundreds of hours durating.17 Although the CL emission of these CL systems can last for a few to hundreds of hours, the light intensity exhibits a peak shape change with duration time without plateau period, which is imperfect for CL assay, especially for real-time bioimaging analysis.
Nanozymes referring to a kind of nanomaterials with enzymatic activity have been used widely in bioassay due to their charming feature of environmental tolerance over natural enzymes.18,19 Among them, the peroxidase nanozymes are well developed, including various inorganic $\mathrm{NPs}^{20}$ and the hemin functionalized nanomaterials.21 Besides, metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) based peroxidase mimics have also been prepared by using active center as node. MOF exhibits high catalytic activity because each active center in MOF can exist in the form of monomer and its active site is accessible by substrates due to the inherent rigid and porous structure of MOF. Except for the applications in biomimetic catalysis, drug delivery, photodynamic therapy, and biosensing,22-25 MOFs have also been used to catalysis CL systems.26-28 Unfortunately, the MOFs-based CL systems show flash-type emission.
In this work, by combining iron porphyrin MOF (FePorMOF) with natural glucose oxidase (GOx), a nano/bio-dual-enzymes design is proposed to develop the intensive and persistent CL system. The bienzyme system in which the substrate of one enzymatic reaction is produced in situ by another exists widely in organism to carry out physiological processes. Particularly when the two enzymes are confined within a nanoscale range, the overall efficiency of enzymatic reactions can be remarkably amplified by reducing the diffusion barrier.29-31 For example, by modifying both HRP and GOx on a quantum dot, a bienzyme-quantum dot conjugate has been prepared for enhancing the chemiluminescence resonance energy transfer efficiency and extending the luminous time to 1 hour.32 The presented nano/bio-dual-enzymes is fabricated by direct adsorption of GOx on FePorMOF with high peroxidase catalytic activity. In the presence of luminol and glucose, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is generated in situ by GOx and consumed immediately by FePorMOF for the CL reaction. The local tandem catalytic reactions by FePorMOF/GOx avoid the diffusion-limited kinetics and enhance the overall catalytic efficiency, leading to a strong CL intensity. In addition, the in situ enzymatic production of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ along with the surface diffusion effect makes the nano/bio-enzymes-based CL system exhibits a long persistent CL emission with a plateau period of 7.5 hours. Based on this CL system, CL imaging analysis methods have been successfully constructed for glucose sensing and homogeneous real-time immunoassay of $\mathfrak{a}$ -fetoprotein (AFP). The proposed nano/bioenzymes-based CL system shows advantages of intensity, long persistence, good biocompatibility and stability, making it a good detection tool for biosensing and bioimaging assay.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Materials and Reagents. $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ , N, N-Dimethylformamide (DMF) were purchased from Aladdin biochemical technology Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). Fetcpp was obtained from Frontier Scientific (USA). GOx, thrombin, HRP, N-Hydroxysuccinimide (NHS), and N-(3- Dimethylaminopropyl)-N'-ethylcarbodiimide hydrochloride (EDC) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). Glucose and pyrogallic acid were purchased from Macklin biochemical technology Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). GOx assay kit (Fluorometric) were obtained from Abcam (Shanghai China). Luminol ( ${\it\Omega}^{\prime}{\sim}2{\mathrm{\mM}})$ ) was provided by Autobio Diagnostics Co. Ltd. (Zhengzhou, China). AFP and corresponding antibodies (clone Nos. Ab1 9K5# and Ab2 102K7#) were purchased from Keybiotech Co. Ltd. (Beijing, China). All other reagents were of analytical grade and used without further purification.
Apparatus. The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images were obtained by a JSM-7800F scanning electron microscope (JEOL Ltd., Japan). Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns were obtained using a D8 ADVANCE XRD instrument (Bruker AXS, Germany). Fouriertransform infrared (FTIR) spectrum was acquired on a Nicolet 6700 spectrometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific, USA). UV-vis absorption values were obtained on a Varioskan Flash spectral scanning multimode reader (Thermo Fisher Scientific, USA). Zeta potential and dynamic light scattering (DLS) analysis were performed on a 90 Plus/BI-MAS equipment (Brookhaven Instruments Co., USA). CL spectrum and Fluorescence measurements were performed on an F-7000 fluorescence spectrophotometer (Hitachi, Japan). The CL kinetics curves were obtained by an MPI-A multifunctional electrochemical and chemiluminescent analytical system (Xi’an Remex Analytical Instrument Co., Ltd. China) with a PMT 300. The CL images were collected through a cooled low-light CCD camera (BioSpectrum 615 Imaging System, UVP, USA). For image analysis, a square was first defined with side length around each spot center, then the mean pixel intensity within the square was used for the CL intensity calculation. Synthesis of FePorMOF. FePorMOF was synthesized according to previous work with little modification.33 Briefly, $15\mathrm{\mg\ZrCl_{4}}$ , $20~\mathrm{mg}$ Fetcpp and $0.4~\mathrm{mL}$ $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O H}$ were dissolved in $5~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF with ultrasonic. Then the mixture was heated at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{{h}}$ in a reaction kettle. After cooling to room temperature, the brown solid FePorMOF was obtain through centrifugation washing with DMF and subsequent drying under vacuum at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The obtained FePorMOF was stored away from light.
Preparation of FePorMOF/GOx. The FePorMOF/GOx was prepared by mixing $1\mathrm{mg/mL}$ FePorMOF and $5\mathrm{mg/mL}$ GOx aqueous solution in equal volume, followed with slight shaking at room temperature for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After centrifugation washing, the FePorMOF/GOx was dispersed in ultrapure water and stored at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ away from light for further use.
Fabrication of FePorMOF/Ab1/BSA. By considering the fabrication universality for different antibodies, FePorMOF/Ab1/BSA was prepared by covalent bonding. Firstly, 1 $\mathrm{mg/mL}$ FePorMOF was reacted with the mixture of $0.4\mathrm{~M~}$ EDC and 0.1 M NHS for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ to activate the carboxyl group on its surface. After washing, $1\mathrm{mg/mL}$ activated FePorMOF was reacted with $1\mathrm{mg/mL}$ Ab1 (capture antibody of AFP) for $12\mathrm{{h}}$ at room temperature with slight shaking. Then the FePorMOF/Ab1 was washed and blocked with BSA $\mathrm{\langle5\mg/mL\rangle}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ at room temperature with slight shaking. After centrifugation washing, the FePorMOF/Ab1/BSA was obtained and stored at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ away from light before use. Here, PBS (10 mM, pH 7.4) was used for washing, reaction and storage.
Catalytic Activity Test. According to the previous works,34,35 pyrogallol- $\mathrm{.H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ reaction was used to evaluate the peroxidase-like catalytic activity of Fetcpp and FePorMOF. The catalytic reaction was carried out with $4~{\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}}$ $(5~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ Fetcpp or $6~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ (The active sites were equivalent to ${5\upmu\mathrm{M}}$ Fetcpp) FePorMOF at different concentrations of pyrogallol (Fetcpp: 5, 10, 20, 25, and $40~\mathrm{mM}$ ; PorMOF: 5, 10, 15, 20, and $35~\mathrm{mM}$ ) and $10\mathrm{mM}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . The increase of the product absorbance in the first 50 s was monitored by UV-Vis spectroscopy at $420\mathrm{nm}$ in the kinetic mode with 2 s intervals. The reaction progress followed a conventional enzymatic dynamic regulation expressed by Michaelis-Menten equation. The Michaelis constant $K_{\mathrm{M}}$ and catalytic constant $K_{\mathrm{cat}}$ were calculated by the Lineweaver-Burk plot (eq 1).
$$
\frac{1}{V}=\frac{K_{M}~1}{V_{m a x}[S]}+\frac{1}{V_{m a x}}
$$
Glucose Detection. Glucose of different concentrations was mixed with FePorMOF/GOx and luminol in which the final concentrations of FePorMOF/GOx and luminol were 0.2 $\mathrm{mg/mL}$ and $0.8\mathrm{mM}$ , respectively. The mixture was added in a 96-well plate and immediately used for CL image collection by CCD with an exposure time of $15\mathrm{min}$ .
AFP Detection. AFP of different concentrations were mixed with the reaction solution containing $0.1~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ FePorMOF/Ab1/BSA, $0.1\ \upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ Ab2, $0.25~{\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}}$ GOx, $0.4~\mathrm{mM}$ luminol, and $5\mathrm{mM}$ glucose. The mixture was added in a 96-well plate and immediately used for continuous CL image collection by CCD. Each CL image was recorded with an exposure time of $10\mathrm{min}$ .
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Nano/bio-enzymes based CL System. The principle of the nano/bio-enzymes CL system using FePorMOF/GOx bioconjugate was illustrated in Figure 1A. In the presence of glucose, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was in situ generated by GOx and then immediately oxidized luminol with FePorMOF to emit blue light. The FePorMOF/GOx bioconjugate could confine the CL reaction on the local surface of FePorMOF via the cascaded $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ generation and consumption. Because the nano/bio-enzymes CL system evaded the diffusion-limited kinetics, the overall catalytic efficiency was amplified to generate strong CL emission over 9 hours (Figure 1B). In addition, the image analysis showed the nano/bio-enzymes system could emit strong luminescence initially and the intensity would maintain at the plateau value over 7.5 hours (Figure 1C). Such persistent CL emission with a long plateau period of several hours should be mainly attributed to the controlled $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ supply by the in situ enzymatic reaction.32
The kinetic curve of the CL reaction of FePorMOF/GOx, luminol and glucose illustrated the CL intensity of the proposed nano/bio-enzymes system underwent four stages: (I) rapid rise to the peak value, (II) fall to the plateau, (III) long lasting at plateau and (IV) decay to background (Figure 2). The behavior of this kinetic curve should be attributed to both the enzymatic reaction kinetics and substrate diffusion kinetics. In the stage I, as the concentrations of both luminol and glucose around FePorMOF/GOx were consistent with their bulk concentrations, the cascaded enzymatic reactions were mainly carried out to generate strong CL, thus, the CL intensity increased rapidly to a peak. Due to the complete consumption of luminol and glucose, the substrates around FePorMOF/GOx should be supplied by diffusion from the bulk solution. Thus, in the stage II the concentrations of substrates were lower than those in stage I, hence the CL intensity fell from the peak. When the diffusion kinetics and enzymatic reaction kinetics reached equilibrium, a plateau was obtained (stage III). The kinetic curve showed a plateau for $440~\mathrm{min}$ , which was consistent with $7.5\mathrm{~h~}$ of the image analysis results (Figure 1C). With the passage of time, luminol was gradually used up, leading to the decay of CL (IV). The exhaustion of luminol was verified by the recovery of CL intensity with extra addition of luminol (data not shown).
Characterization of FePorMOF. FePorMOF was synthesized by using Fetcpp as linker and zirconium ion as node via solvothermal reaction (Figure 3A). The SEM image showed the as prepared FePorMOFs were uniform in a rod-like structure with a $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ length and 150 nm width (Figure 3B). One thing to be noted, some FePorMOF surface had flakes, it should be from the uneven growth of MOF because the heating and cooling process would not be uniform absolutely. The FT-IR characterization of FePorMOF showed the typical porphine ring skeleton absorptions at 1714 (w), 1606 (s) and 1419 (vs), and the O-H stretch absorption at 999 (s), indicating the involvement of Fetcpp in the frameworks (Figure S1A). Additionally, the PXRD curve showed the peaks at 4.87o, $7.15^{\mathrm{{\circ}}}$ , $8.30^{\mathrm{o}}$ , and $9.69^{\mathrm{o}}$ , which could be indexed to (-220), (410), (500), and (-150) of the spindle haxahedron geometry, respectively (Figure S1B).33 All the above results confirmed the successful synthesis of FePorMOF.
The CL generation of luminal- $\mathbf{\cdotH}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ by FePorMOF was studied. The CL spectra showed the emission peaks of luminal- $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ catalyzed by FePorMOF, Fetcpp and HRP were all at $423\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 3C). This result indicated the CL mechanism of luminol- $\mathrm{.H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -FePorMOF was similar as that of luminol- $\mathbf{\cdotH}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ -HRP, in which FePorMOF catalyzed the oxidization of luminol to generate luminescence.
The catalytic parameters of FePorMOF and Fetcpp36 were measured through the oxidation reaction of pyrogallol- $\mathrm{.H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . The Michaelis constant $K_{\mathrm{M}}$ of $3.20\mathrm{mM}$ and the catalytic constant $K_{\mathrm{cat}}$ of $4.99\ \mathrm{min^{-1}}$ were obtained for FePorMOF from the Lineweaver-Burk plot (Figure 3D and Table S1). Compared with Fetcpp, the FePorMOF processed higher $K_{\mathrm{cat}}$ value and lower $K_{\mathrm{M}}$ value, indicating its higher catalytic activity and affinity. The higher $K_{\mathrm{cat}}/K_{\mathrm{M}}$ value also indicated the higher catalytic efficiency of FePorMOF than Fetcpp which might be due to the rigid and porous structure of MOF. FePorMOF could also be used for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ detection because of its good catalytic ability (Figure S2).
The environmental tolerance of the catalytic ability was an important criterion of enzyme. Here, the effects of pH and temperature on the catalytic activity of FePorMOF were investigated (Figure 3E and F). For FePorMOF, the CL intensity gradually increased when the pH of the reaction solution changed from acidic to alkaline (Figure 3E). This was because FePorMOF could maintain its activity in alkaline solution which was more suitable for the luminol- $\mathbf{\cdotH}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ reaction.37 However, for both Fetcpp and HRP CL system, no significant change of CL intensity was observed when pH was higher than 8.0, indicating the partial loss of catalytic activity of Fetcpp and HRP in alkaline solution. One thing to be noted, the color of Fetcpp stock solution would change visually in alkaline solution due to the structural failure of Fetcpp. The temperature tolerance experiment showed both FePorMOF and Fetcpp could retain their activity even at $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . In contrast, HRP was extremely sensitive to temperature, it would loss $48\%$ activity at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and completely deactivate at $90^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure 3F). All the above results indicated the high stability of FePorMOF nanozyme than the natural HRP in alkaline and high temperature environment.
Preparation of FePorMOF/GOx. FePorMOF/GOx bioconjugate was fabricated by electrostatic adsorption of electronegative GOx on the electropositive surface of FePorMOF. The hydration diameter analysis showed both FePorMOF and FePorMOF/GOx had two peak positions which were attributed to the horizontal and longitudinal diameter of the rod-like structure (Figure 4A). The two peaks of FePorMOF were at 140 and $532~\mathrm{nm}$ , which was consistent with the result in SEM. In contrast to FePorMOF, the hydration diameters of
FePorMOF/GOx extended to 160 and $541~\mathrm{nm}$ , indicating the modification of GOx on the surface of FePorMOF. Meanwhile, the zeta potential experiment confirmed the electronegativity and electropositivity of GOx and FePorMOF (Figure 4B). Compared the zeta potential of $54.6~\mathrm{mv}$ of FePorMOF, the zeta potential of FePorMOF/GOx fell to $1.7~\mathrm{mv}$ , confirming the modification of GOx on FePorMOF.
The modification amount of GOx on FePorMOF was calculated by GOx detection kit (Figure 4C). By subtracting the FL intensity of supernatant solution after adsorption from the FL intensity of GOx reaction solution, $0.92\mathrm{mg}$ GOx was calculated to be absorbed on $1~\mathrm{mg}$ FePorMOF. This modification capacity was comparable to the direct physical encapsulation.38
Adjustment of the CL Duration Time. The persistent time of CL system is significant for CL bioassay and imaging. In the present work, the nano/bio-enzymes system used glucose and luminol as substrates. As the physiological concentration of glucose was $\sim5~\mathrm{mM}$ , the CL emission of FePorMOF/GOx-glucose-luminol was examined at different luminol concentrations with $5~\mathrm{mM}$ glucose. The CL images showed the FePorMOF/GOx-glucoseluminol system could express strong emission to achieve the CCD exposure intensity threshold even with $0.2~\mathrm{mM}$ luminol, and the CL duration time extended from 160 to $460\ \mathrm{min}$ with increasing the luminol concentration from 0.2 to $1.2\mathrm{mM}$ (Figure 5A). Except the whole CL lasting time, the corresponding image intensity analysis showed the plateau period also increased with increasing the luminol concentration (Figure 5B). The adjustment of the CL duration time also examined with the kinetic curves (Figure S3), which showed the CL emission possessed plateau periods in all luminol concentrations and the plateau period extending with the increase of luminol concentrations. These results indicated the CL persistent time, along with the plateau period, of the FePorMOF/GOx-glucose-luminol system could be adjusted by luminol concentrations. However, because the alkalinity of reaction solution would increase with the increase of luminol concentration, a relatively luminol concentration of $0.4\mathrm{mM}$ used in the following experiments if there was no special explanation.
Persistent CL Performance. The CL performance of the proposed FePorMOF/GOxglucose-luminol was compared with other CL systems using non-local bienzymes or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ substrate (Figure 6). In this experiment, the glucose used the physiological concentration of 5 mM, and luminol used $0.4~\mathrm{mM}$ , assuring the luminol could be all oxidized for luminescence. The CL images showed the CL duration time of the proposed FePorMOF/GOx-glucoseluminol system (a) was $210~\mathrm{min}$ , which would be decreased to 50 or $80~\mathrm{min}$ when using directly the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (b: FePorMOF/GOx-luminol- $\mathrm{.H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ ) or non-local bienzymes (c: FePorMOFGOx-glucose-luminol) (Figure 6A). This was because the FePorMOF/GOx-glucose-luminol system could not only make the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production controlled by enzyme reaction, but also make the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -luminol reaction controlled by the diffusion, leading to the continuous oxidization of luminol for long persistent CL. In addition, the CL duration time of FePorMOF- $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ - luminol system (d) was $20~\mathrm{min}$ , which was much shorter than $50~\mathrm{min}$ of FePorMOF/GOxluminol- $\mathbf{\cdotH}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ . This should be attributed to the hindrance of GOx on FePorMOF, confirming the slower catalytic CL reaction could lead to longer CL duration time. The extension of CL emission time by in situ enzymatic $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production was also observed in Fetcpp (e) and HRP (g) based CL systems, however, in these systems the two catalyst were molecules, thus, the
CL duration time could only be $20\mathrm{min}$ due to the lack of diffusion control of the homogeneous CL reaction.
The kinetic curves also confirmed only the proposed FePorMOF/GOx-glucose-luminol system (a) could possess a persistent CL emission with long plateau period (Figure 6B). Assuming all enzymes had $100\%$ reaction efficiency, the total photons should be same in all these systems due to the same amount of luminol. The kinetic curves showed all the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ triggered CL reactions presented a fast fading CL performance, however, the attenuation velocity of FePorMOF system was lower than Fetcpp and HRP, indicating FePorMOF was a suitable catalyst for developing grow-type CL. In contrast, all the bi-enzyme CL systems presented a relaxation luminescence, indicating the in situ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production could delay the oxidization of luminol for luminescence. All the results indicated, based on the local double enzyme design，the FePorMOF/GOx performed an excellent persistent CL system with long plateau period through controlling the oxidization of luminol by both enzymatic reaction and substrate diffusion.
Stability of FePorMOF/GOx. The stability of FePorMOF/GOx was examined through FePorMOF/GOx-glucose-luminol CL system. In this experiment, the mixture of $0.1~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ FePorMOF/GOx, $1.2~\mathrm{mM}$ luminol and $5\mathrm{mM}$ glucose was placed in a covered 96-well plate but not in the sealed PCR tube (used in Figure 1) for CL imaging, thus, the CL persistent time was slightly different from Figure 1. Although an obvious decrease of the duration time could be obtained after a storage of 19 days, the duration time of FePorMOF/GOx-glucose-luminol system was 340 min with a plateau period of 250 min even after a storage of 33 days (Figure 7A). In addition, the persistent CL trend of the FePorMOF/GOx-glucose-luminol system was kept during the whole storage period (Figure 7B). All these results indicated the good catalytic stability of FePorMOF/GOx.
CL Imaging Assay of Glucose and AFP. The proposed FePorMOF/GOx-glucoseluminol system was used for CL imaging assay of glucose (Figure 8A). The CL intensity increased obviously with increasing the concentration of glucose, and a good linear correlation between the CL intensity and glucose concentration in the range from 50 to $1000~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ was obtained. The limit of detection (LOD) of glucose at 3SD was $39.2\upmu\mathrm{M}$ .
The results in Figure 6 showed both the FePorMOF/GOx-luminol- $\mathbf{\cdotH}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and FePorMOFGOx-glucose-luminol systems could generate CL emission more than 1 h due to the surface hindrance of FePorMOF and in situ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ enzymatic production, respectively. Hence, we designed a FePorMOF/Ab1/BSA-GOx-luminol-Glucose CL system for simple homogenous
CL imaging assay of AFP in which the detection was based on the sandwich immunoreactioninduced surface hindrance of FePorMOF and the CL system also kept the enzymatic generation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . In the presence of target, sandwich immunocomplexes were formed on the surface of FePorMOF, which hinder the diffusion of substrates including enzymatic produced $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and luminol to the surface of FePorMOF,39 resulting in the decrease of the CL intensity (Figure S4). The CL intensity and the logarithm of AFP concentration showed a linear relationship within $0.5{\cdot}1000~\mathrm{ng/mL}$ , with a correlation coefficient of 0.9554 (Figure 8B). Furthermore, due to its long-lasting CL characteristics, the real-time detection performance of the FePorMOF/Ab1/BSA-GOx-luminol-Glucose CL immunoassay system was examined by continuous capture of CL intensity within $180~\mathrm{{min}}$ (Figure S5). For all AFP concentrations, the CL intensity decreased gradually with time in the first $80~\mathrm{min}$ , but after that, the CL intensity of AFP concentration higher than $10~\mathrm{{ng/mL}}$ continued to decrease, while the CL intensity of AFP concentration less than or equal to $10\ \mathrm{ng/mL}$ showed a recovery. We speculated the turning point 80 min was the saturation immune time. After the complete immunoreaction on FePorMOF, the persistent CL performance could continue to occur in low AFP concentrations due to the small surface hindrance.
Finally, the specificity of the FePorMOF/Ab1/BSA-GOx-luminol-Glucose CL immunoassay system was evaluated (Figure 8C). Compared with AFP, there was no significant change in CL intensity after adding BSA and thrombin, which indicated the proposed CL immunoassay had a good specificity for proteins. However, because this CL imaging immunoassay was based on the sandwich immunoreaction-induced surface diffusion inhibition, the detection of AFP in actual serum sample may not be very optimistic. All-in-one CL probe which packaged GOx and CL substrate in FePorMOF would be developed in the follow-up experiment for immunoassay.
## CONCLUSION
In summary, we have demonstrated an intensive and persistent CL system based on a nano/bioenzymes conjugate of FePorMOF/GOx. FePorMOF nanozyme showed good peroxidase catalytic activity and environmental tolerance. The FePorMOF/GOx could generate strong CL emission over 9 hours with glucose and luminol substrates due to its high catalytic efficiency based on the local tandem catalysis of CL reaction on the surface of FePorMOF. Importantly, a long constant CL emission of 7.5 hours was observed for the first time in the FePorMOF/GOx-glucose-luminol system through controlling the CL reaction by in situ enzymatic $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ supply and substrate diffusion. In addition, the CL persistent time could be adjusted by luminol concentrations. Similarly, the long-lasting CL emission was also found in FePorMOF/Ab1/BSA-GOx-luminol-Glucose system. Based on the persistent CL system, a one-step homogeneous immunoassay was designed for continuous kinetic sensing of AFP. The proposed nano/bio-dual enzymes CL system characterized with strong intensity, long constant emission, good stability and physiological adaptation, showed potential applications in real-time bioassay and in vivo imaging.
